 "summary": "Prohibits the head of the office for people with developmental disabilities from changing the auspice of any individualized residential alternative that is operated by the state.",
 "title": "Prohibits the head of the office for people with developmental disabilities from changing the auspice of any individualized residential alternative that is operated by the state",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "S8200",
     "basePrintNoStr": "S8200-2017",
     "printNo": "S8200",
     "session": 2017,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 323\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 8200, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to prohibiting\n      the head of the office for people with developmental  disabilities\n      from changing the auspice of any individualized residential alter-\n      native that is operated by the state\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  bill  would  prohibit  the  Office of Persons with Developmental\nDisabilities (OPWDD) from changing  the  management  of  state  operated\nIndividualized  Residential  Alternatives  (IRAs).  I  am constrained to\ndisapprove this bill for both public policy and fiscal reasons.\n \n  First, this bill would be detrimental to persons with disabilities who\nare served by OPWDD. Changing the management of problematic  IRAs  is  a\ncritical  quality assurance tool that OPWDD deploys when an IRA exhibits\npoor performance. I will  not  limit  OPWDD's  ability  to  take  action\nagainst poor performing state service providers.\n \n  Second,  interfering  with  the  State's  management of IRA operations\nwould jeopardize the state fiscal plan and infringe upon agreements made\nbetween the Legislature and the Executive during budgetary negotiations.\nAdditionally, the State must have the flexibility  to  deploy  resources\nthat  best  meet  the  needs  of  the  New Yorkers reliant upon critical\nservices while ensuring that tax dollars are invested as efficiently  as\npossible. This legislation limits that flexibility.\n \n  Finally,  this  legislation  revisits  issues  addressed during budget\nnegotiations. Recent budget agreements have included  requirements  that\nOPWDD provide the employees with significant notice prior to any manage-\nment  change  or  closure.  Any  further  changes  in  the law should be\naddressed in the context of the annual state  budget  negotiations.  For\nthe foregoing reasons, I am obligated to veto this bill today.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
